                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "NOTE: This is just a first impressions review. Due to everyone having issues posting a review currently I am writing this as more of a test and first impressions review rather than an in depth review at this time.--\n\nThe game drops you in with a tutorial and right away you qucikly realize that this is going to be a puzzle game that feels like it was inspired by the legendary Tetris at first glance. Except this game has something else to it rather than just dropping puzzle pieces down to get you to make them disappear. You are playing as a space ship commander that is on a mission to retrieve energy to save the sun, which makes your puzzling a bit more interesting.\n\nThe puzzle pieces dropping are combined to do specific maneuvers and powers. You combine them in a way to move your ship, destroy asteroids, or pick up some cargo. This makes it pretty fun to have this extra thought process going on while trying to avoid having the pieces reach the top for a game over. \n\nYou start in a \"Story\" mode and do missions that have you collecting energy to save the sun. On the left side of the puzzle screen you will be shown combos that you have to use to navigate your ship through whatever obstacles are shown at the bottom of the screen, or blast away at things in your way. As the pieces drop you must flip them and place them in a way that matches these combos to achieve the correct action for whatever it is your ship needs to do. It is simply explained by your friend \"Pea\" who makes it wasy for you in the tutorial without any enemies being around. As you adveance further more obstacles appear to make you have to think more aobut your end goals. Of course matching pieces that don't perform actions is important too for combos and filling up your special move meter. \n\nAs I said this is a quck first glance so I can't say much aobut more advanced game modes, but my first impressions are that this is a fun puzzle game with some new ideas in the \"falling blocks\" genre of puzzle games. Based on the few missions I played I should also note that you are able to collect in game currency that allows you to buy ship upgrades, better ships, and different commanders. So there appears to be some extra collectible and RPG elements to this as well. \n\nGamepad support works fine for me and I have had no issues with it through the first few missions. The game also has 9 Trading cards, for which you get 5 drops as well as Steam Achievements.",
